    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 281008\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n208 AM PST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Expect warming temperatures and generally light breezes through \n  the weekend into the start of next week.\n\n* An incoming system will bring chances of gusty winds, widespread\n  rain, and mountain snow to the region early-to-mid week.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Friday.\n\nWeather will remain generally quiet through the region this weekend \nthanks to an area of high pressure in the southeastern Pacific \nOcean. Temperatures will continue to range 8 to 10 degrees above \nseasonal normals through the weekend into the start of next week. \nWinds will remain generally light across the region this weekend. \nChances on Sunday of gusty west winds of 40 mph or greater making it \ndown the eastern slopes of the Sierra into the Owens Valley have \ndropped below 30 percent. Similarly, chances of gusty west winds in \nthe western Mojave Desert have dropped to 30 to 50 percent, heavily \nfavoring the higher terrain of northwestern San Bernardino County \novernight Sunday into Monday. Opted out of any Wind Advisory \nissuances, but will continue to assess the need for one over the \nnext 24 hours.\n\nUnsettled weather returns next week as a closed area of low pressure \npushes into the southern California Coast from the eastern Pacific \nOcean. This system's path has shifted slightly farther south, but is \ngenerally on-track among the various ensemble means for the center \nof the low to move inland north of San Diego. Chances for gusty pre-\nfrontal southerly winds on Tuesday continue to decrease and shift \neastward, with impacts largely confined to southern Mohave County; \nhowever, chances of gusty post-frontal north-northwesterly winds \nTuesday night into Wednesday have increased, with a 30 to 50 percent \nchance of 40+ mph gusts across Inyo, Esmeralda, central Nye \ncounties... lower chances farther south. Precipitation will push \ninto the southern Great Basin Monday night into Tuesday morning with \nsnow levels generally between 7000 and 8000 feet before pushing \neastward through the day on Tuesday. Due to the southward shift of \nthe low, forecast snow amounts continue to decrease in the eastern \nSierra, with now a 30-40% chance of 8+ inches in Aspendell (8400 ft).\nMoisture associated with this system will be substantial, as the \ncenter of the low will track over the southeastern Pacific Ocean, \nwhere sea-surface temperatures are around 10 degrees Fahrenheit \nabove-average, setting the stage for prolific moisture transport. \nAdditionally, the southerly trajectory of the flow as the low pushes \ninto southern California will circumvent many mountain ranges that \ntend to rain-shadow desert valleys, resulting in PWATs approaching \n300 percent pushing into southeastern California, southern Nevada, \nand northwestern Arizona. That said, though confidence is \nincreasing, there remains a substantial spread in forecast total \nprecipitation amounts, with a 90% chance of 0.35 inches and a 10% \nchance of over 2 inches in desert valleys. Snow amounts in the \nSpring Mountains will largely be confined to locations above 7500 \nfeet, with a 70-80% chance of 8+ inches at Lee Canyon and a 20-30% \nat Kyle Canyon. With any shifts in the track of this system, expect \nQPF fluctuations. \n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds \nremain light and generally follow typical, daily patterns throughout \nthe TAF period. Chance of seeing 10+ knots is under 10%. Scattered \nto broken clouds at or above 15kft.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light and diurnal winds \nacross most of the region today. Northerly breezes linger in the \nLower Colorado River Valley, but any gusts should remain below 20 \nknots. Scattered to broken clouds at or above 15kft. \n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
